course: dictionary
lesson: 13397
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Hem is one of them, so context tells you which meaning is right. «Mum showed me how to hem my new skirt» is about sewing. «The hem of her dress was muddy» means the bottom edge of the cloth.
explain: Look at the words around hem. «We were hemmed in by the crowd» means we could not move, because hemmed in means surrounded. «Ahem! Listen to me» is a small cough, not something about cloth. Ask yourself: which meaning fits here?
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If the clues are about sewing, walls or a cough, then that is the meaning. When you are still not sure, a dictionary will settle it.
fact: Hem can be a noun, a verb, or an interjection.
fact: A hem is the edge of a piece of cloth, folded over and sewn so it does not fray.
fact: To hem means to sew a hem on something.
fact: «Hemmed in» means surrounded so you cannot move or escape.
fact: «Ahem!» is a little cough to get attention or show doubt, and «hem and haw» means to hesitate and not decide.
exercise: «The hem of her skirt was torn.» What is a hem? | A kind of sharp needle | A small cough | The middle of a hat | * The folded, sewn edge of a piece of cloth
exercise: «We were hemmed in by the crowd.» What does hemmed in mean? | Cleaned very carefully | Cooked in a pan | Happy and cheerful | * Surrounded so we could not move
exercise: «Ahem! Listen to me,» said the teacher. Why did she say ahem? | To ask for lunch | To say she was happy | Because she was sneezing | * To get everyone's attention
test: Mum will hem my trousers this weekend. What will Mum do? | Cut them into shorts | * Sew a folded edge on them | Give them to a shop | Dye them a new colour
test: «Tom hemmed and hawed before he picked a biscuit.» What does hemmed and hawed mean? | Ate very fast | Laughed loudly | Coughed to get attention | * Hesitated and could not decide
exam: «I need to hem my sleeve before the party.» What does hem most likely mean here? | To surround the sleeve so it cannot move | To cough loudly to get attention | * To sew a neat edge on the sleeve | To hesitate about going to the party
